        "**Who collected it, for what purpose, what is missing and why?**\n",
        "\n",
        "*Your notes here.*\n",
        "\n",
        "**ACS (S1701, S1501, B01002)**\n",
        "\n",
        "The U.S. Census Bureau collects this data through the American Community Survey (ACS). I use the 2020–2024 5-year estimates. The ACS provides information about communities between decennial censuses and helps allocate federal funding. Each county value is a survey estimate based on five years of data, not a full count. It also includes a 90% margin of error (MOE). Small counties can have very large MOEs. For example, Loving County, TX (33 people) has a bachelor’s share of 0% ± 66.1 percentage points.\n",
        "\n",
        "**RUCC 2023**\n",
        "\n",
        "The USDA Economic Research Service classifies counties into 9 codes based on metro area size, urban population, and proximity to a metro area. Codes 1–3 represent metro counties, while codes 4–9 represent nonmetro counties. The classification uses the 2020 Census and 2023 OMB metro definitions. RUCC is a classification, not a measurement, so its codes are categories rather than a numerical scale.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What is missing and why?**\n",
        "\n",
        "- There are no missing values in the final sample of 3,144 counties.\n",
        "- I excluded these areas because my research covers only the 50 states and DC:\n",
        "  - 78 Puerto Rico municipios\n",
        "  - 13 RUCC records for other U.S. territories (American Samoa, Guam, Northern Mariana Islands, and U.S. Virgin Islands)\n",
        "- Some missing data may still exist within the ACS. The Census Bureau uses weighting and imputation to account for households that do not respond. However, if poorer households are less likely to respond, the poverty estimates may still be biased."
      ],
