        "*Your words here.*\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**The model**\n",
        "\n",
        "Table 3 shows the OLS results for county poverty rates. Column (1) includes only bachelor’s share. Columns (2) to (4) add state dummies, RUCC dummies, and median age one by one. Column (4) is my main model. Standard errors are clustered by state.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpreting the bachelor’s-share coefficient**\n",
        "\n",
        "Compare two counties that share the same state, rural-urban status, and median age. If the percentage of bachelor's degree holders in a county is 1 percentage point higher, the poverty rate in that region is on average 0.19 percentage points lower.\n",
        "\n",
        "The 95% confidence interval is 0.15 percentage points lower than 0.22 percentage points, indicating that this relationship is statistically significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "Counties with a bachelor’s degree rate one standard deviation higher (10.3) are about 1.9 points lower in poverty. This is about 13% of the average county poverty rate of 14.2%. This control reduces the coefficient by about 30% at temperature (1), making it -0.27.\n",
        "\n",
        "This suggests that some of the underlying relationships in Figure 2 stem not only from education but also from state-level and rural–urban differences. However, this is merely an association, not causation. College graduates may already be relocating to places with better job opportunities.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpreting the median-age coefficient**\n",
        "\n",
        "Holding education level, rural-to-urban ratio, and state constant, counties with a higher median age have a poverty rate 0.29 percentage points lower (95% CI: 0.20–0.37 percentage points lower). Counties with 1 standard deviation (5.4 years) more are about 1.5 percentage points lower in poverty rate.\n",
        "\n",
        "One possible reason could be that the older the county, the more retirees receive Social Security benefits and remain above the poverty line. They also tend to have fewer children, and those children have the highest poverty rates.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpreting a RUCC coefficient**\n",
        "\n",
        "Counties in the most remote category (code 9) have poverty rates 4.8 percentage points higher than large metro counties (code 1), at the same education level and median age (SE = 0.62). This suggests that rural areas have higher poverty for reasons beyond differences in education.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Justifying each control**\n",
        "\n",
        "- **State dummies: control for confounding factors.** States have different policies and living costs that affect both education and poverty. Tuition policies and university systems affect education levels, while Medicaid expansion, minimum wages, and state EITCs affect poverty. Without state dummies, the education coefficient could reflect these state differences. State is not a bad control because education cannot change which state a county belongs to. I also cluster standard errors by state because counties in the same state share many policies.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **RUCC dummies: control for rural–urban differences.** Rural areas tend to have fewer jobs that require a degree and offer lower wages. This can lead to both lower education levels and higher poverty. Without RUCC, the education coefficient may be too negative. I use eight dummy variables because RUCC codes are categories, not a numerical scale (Figure 3). However, RUCC could be a bad control if higher education helps a county grow and become more urban. In that case, rurality is partly a mediator between education and poverty. Controlling for it would remove part of the relationship. For this reason, column (7) of Table 4 excludes RUCC. The coefficient changes from -0.19 to -0.25. The first estimate compares counties with similar rurality, while the second also includes the part of the relationship related to urbanization.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Median age: controls for age differences and improves precision.** Retirement areas and older farming counties may have different education levels. Age also affects poverty because of Social Security and differences in the number of children. Adding median age increases R^2 from 0.425 to 0.476 between columns (3) and (4). This means age explains some additional variation in poverty. However, age could be a bad control if college graduates move into an area and lower its median age. The education coefficient barely changes after adding age (-0.182 to -0.186), so this does not seem to have a major effect on my results.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Robustness (Table 4)**\n",
        "\n",
        "The main model assigns the same weight to all counties. My question is focused on the county. In the fifth column, weights were applied to counties based on population to focus on the average person. The coefficient is -0.15; it is smaller but still negative and statistically significant. Even after excluding Loving County in the 6th row, the coefficient remained almost unchanged (-0.187). Finally, use log poverty as the outcome variable for the eighth column. The poverty rate was found to decrease by about 1.5 percentage points for every 1 percentage point increase in the percentage of people holding a bachelor's degree. This suggests that the main outcome is not affected by the distorted distribution of poverty rates shown in Figure 1."
