        "**Constant variance**\n",
        "\n",
        "The results of the Breusch-Pagan test are consistent with the observations made in the residual graph (LM = 435.9, p < 0.001). I reject the assumption that dispersion is constant. In other words, the standard error of the ordinary OLS may be too small.\n",
        "\n",
        "I use the standard error clustered by week. This takes unequal dispersion into account, enabling the interrelation of errors between counties within the same state for analysis. The cluster standard error for the proportion of bachelor's degree holders is 0.017, which is higher than 0.010 for the standard error of ordinary OLS and 0.011 for the HC1 robust standard error. Aggregation reduces the accuracy of estimates, but it is a more cautious approach. Nevertheless, the 95% confidence interval (-0.22 to -0.15) is still far from zero.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Variance inflation factors (VIF)**\n",
        "\n",
        "The VIF of the proportionality is 1.68. In other words, the standard error is about 30% larger than if I had assumed that education was independent of the other variables (√1.68 ≈ 1.3). This is a relatively small increase.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because rural counties tend to have lower levels of education, the level of education overlaps to some extent with the control variable. However, the degree of overlap is not large enough to make the estimate unreliable. The mean VIF is 1.32, and the VIF of the largest RUCC pile is 2.96. All figures are below the typical warning thresholds of 5 or 10. Therefore, multicollinearity does not appear to be a major problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Influential observations**\n",
        "\n",
        "Using Cook’s rule and the 4/N rule (cutoff = 0.0013), I selected 130 influential counties from a total of 3,144. The most influential areas are Ogallala and Todd counties in South Dakota, and Sioux County in North Dakota. These are reservation counties with poverty rates between 43% and 58%, far higher than model projections based on education level, rural status, and state characteristics. Kala Wao County, Hawaii (population 66), also has a high Cook's distance.\n",
